WN.lb(24,{"mt_-c4Ca_nBzX":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A bag has 1 green and {b} blue counters. P(green) = 1/?","answer":"b+1","hint":"Count every counter.","explain":"1 green out of {=b+1} counters: 1/{=b+1}.","params":{"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Ava says: 'It will either rain or not, so P(rain) = 1/2.' What is wrong?","choices":["Rain and no rain are not equally likely","Nothing","It should be 1/3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fractions need equal chances.","explain":"Two outcomes only give 1/2 when they are equally likely."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Where does P(rolling a 7 on a normal die) go on the 0-to-1 scale?","choices":["0","1/7","1/6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can it happen?","explain":"There is no 7 on a die: impossible, so 0."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A bag holds 2 green and 4 blue counters. P(green) is…","choices":["1/3","1/2","2/4"],"answer":0,"hint":"2 out of 6.","explain":"2/6 = 1/3."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A spinner has 24 equal parts. How many must be red for P(red) = 1/{d}?","answer":"24/d","hint":"1/{d} of 24.","explain":"24 ÷ {d} = {=24/d} parts.","params":{"d":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A fair spinner with 3 equal parts landed on red 0 times in 5 spins. Is P(red) now 0?","choices":["No, it is still 1/3; short runs wobble","Yes","It is now 1/2"],"answer":0,"hint":"The spinner did not change.","explain":"P(red) stays 1/3. Over many spins red comes up about a third of the time."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which unit fraction is the smallest chance?","choices":["1/10","1/5","1/2"],"answer":0,"hint":"More equal parts, smaller each.","explain":"1/10 is the smallest: 10 equal chances."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A fair die is rolled. What is P(rolling a 4)?","choices":["1/6","1/4","4/6"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many equally likely faces?","explain":"6 equal faces, one of them is 4: 1/6."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A spinner has {n} equal parts and 1 is red. P(red) = 1/?","answer":"n","hint":"One part out of all the equal parts.","explain":"1 out of {n}: P(red) = 1/{n}.","params":{"n":[3,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A spinner is red and blue, but red covers only a quarter of it. P(red) is…","choices":["1/4","1/2","2/4 because there are 2 colours"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the size, not the colours.","explain":"Red is 1/4 of the circle, so P(red) = 1/4."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A bag has {g} green and {b} blue counters. How many counters are there altogether, the bottom number of P(green)?","answer":"g+b","hint":"Count every counter in the bag.","explain":"{g} + {b} = {=g+b}, so P(green) = {g}/{=g+b}.","params":{"g":[1,4],"b":[2,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Probability tells you how likely something is, from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain).","When all the chances are equal, count them. A coin has 2 equal sides, so heads is 1/2. A die has 6 equal faces, so a 6 is 1/6.","Count equal chances, not colours. A spinner with red and blue is only 1/2 red if the two parts are the same size."],"adult":["For equally likely outcomes, P(event) = favourable outcomes ÷ total outcomes. A single outcome out of n is the unit fraction 1/n, so bigger n means a smaller chance.","A common mistake is to count categories rather than equal chances (\"it either happens or it doesn't, so 1/2\"). Another is to expect short runs to match theory: 5 spins wobble, 1000 spins settle near the fraction."]},"familyTip":"Make a paper spinner with 4 equal parts, colour one red, and spin it 20 times with a pencil and paper clip. How close to 1/4 do you get?","keyIdea":"When every outcome is equally likely, the probability of one outcome is 1 over the number of outcomes: a fair die gives 1/6 for each number.","visual":{"type":"problab","caption":"Roll a die many times. Each face settles near 1/6 of the rolls.","opts":{"kind":"dice"}},"lab":{"type":"flagprob","caption":"Flagship lab: the fairground spinner. Flick it, then reshape it and see the chance change.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_KPo7lOiN6y":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which list could be the probabilities of a probability distribution?","choices":["0.3, 0.3, 0.4","0.5, 0.5, 0.5","0.7, 0.4, −0.1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check both rules: no probability below 0 or above 1, and a sum of exactly 1.","explain":"0.3 + 0.3 + 0.4 = 1 with no negatives. The second sums to 1.5, and the third has a negative probability."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"X is the total of two fair dice. How many of the 36 outcomes give X ≤ {c}?","answer":"(c-1)*c/2","hint":"Add the outcomes for each total: a total of 2 has 1, a total of 3 has 2, and so on, up to {c}.","explain":"1 + 2 + … + {=c-1} = {=(c-1)*c/2} outcomes, so P(X ≤ {c}) = {=(c-1)*c/2}/36.","params":{"c":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"X is the total of two fair dice. What is P(X < 4)?","choices":["3/36","6/36","2/36"],"answer":0,"hint":"“<” is strict, so 4 is not included. Which totals are less than 4?","explain":"Only totals 2 and 3: 1/36 + 2/36 = 3/36. The answer 6/36 is P(X ≤ 4)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Y is the larger score when two fair dice are rolled. What is P(Y = 6)?","choices":["11/36","6/36","1/36"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the cells in the 6 × 6 grid where at least one die shows 6.","explain":"Six cells have a 6 on the first die and six have a 6 on the second. (6, 6) is in both, so there are 6 + 6 − 1 = 11 outcomes and P(Y = 6) = 11/36."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A fair die is rolled {=6*n} times. How many sixes would you expect?","answer":"n","hint":"P(6) = 1/6. Multiply by the number of rolls.","explain":"{=6*n} × 1/6 = {n} sixes. The real count will vary around this.","params":{"n":[5,50]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A spinner landed on red 43 times in 100 spins. Which statement is best?","choices":["0.43 is an estimate of P(red)","P(red) is exactly 0.43","P(red) must be 0.5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a result from data the true probability, or an estimate of it?","explain":"43/100 = 0.43 is a relative frequency. It estimates P(red); more spins give a better estimate."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"P(X = x) = kx for x = 1, 2, 3, 4. Find P(X = {x}).","answer":"x/10","hint":"First find k from the rule that the probabilities sum to 1.","explain":"10k = 1 so k = 0.1, and P(X = {x}) = 0.1 × {x} = {=x/10}.","params":{"x":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In the notation P(X = x), what does the capital X stand for?","choices":["The random variable","One value it can take","The probability"],"answer":0,"hint":"One letter names the variable and the other names a value. Which is which?","explain":"Capital X is the random variable; small x is a particular value, such as 7."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"X is the total of two fair dice. Which value of X is most likely?","choices":["7","12","All values are equally likely"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the pairs of scores that give each total out of the 36 outcomes.","explain":"Seven comes from 6 of the 36 outcomes, more than any other total, so P(X = 7) = 6/36 is the largest."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"X is the total of two fair dice. How many of the 36 equally likely outcomes give X = {t}?","answer":"t-1","hint":"List the pairs (first die, second die) that add to {t}, starting with (1, {=t-1}).","explain":"The pairs are (1, {=t-1}) up to ({=t-1}, 1), which is {=t-1} outcomes, so P(X = {t}) = {=t-1}/36.","params":{"t":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"P(X = x) = kx for x = 1, 2, 3, 4. What is k?","choices":["0.1","0.25","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"The probabilities must sum to 1. Write the sum in terms of k.","explain":"k + 2k + 3k + 4k = 10k = 1, so k = 0.1. The value 0.25 would only work if every probability were equal."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A discrete random variable takes the values 1, 2 and 3. P(X = 1) = 0.{a} and P(X = 2) = 0.{b}. Find P(X = 3).","answer":"(10-a-b)/10","hint":"All the probabilities in a distribution add up to 1.","explain":"P(X = 3) = 1 − 0.{a} − 0.{b} = {=(10-a-b)/10}.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,5]},"where":"a+b<10"}],"explain":{"kid":["Roll two dice and add the scores. The total is a random variable, written with a capital letter: X. A small letter, x, stands for one value it can take. X can be any whole number from 2 to 12.","There are 6 × 6 = 36 equally likely outcomes. Count the ones that give each total. Seven comes from six pairs: (1, 6), (2, 5), (3, 4), (4, 3), (5, 2) and (6, 1). So P(X = 7) = 6/36. A total of 12 comes only from (6, 6), so P(X = 12) = 1/36. The totals are not equally likely.","A table of every value and its probability is the probability distribution. Two rules always hold. Each probability is between 0 and 1, and all of them add to 1. Use the sum to find an unknown. If P(X = x) = kx for x = 1, 2, 3 and 4, then k + 2k + 3k + 4k = 10k = 1, so k = 0.1.","Read the signs with care. P(X ≤ 4) includes 4, so it is 1/36 + 2/36 + 3/36 = 6/36. P(X < 4) leaves 4 out, so it is 3/36. You can also estimate a distribution from data. Roll the dice many times and divide each count by the number of rolls. These relative frequencies get close to the model, but they are rarely exact."],"adult":["This topic is part of the A level Mathematics content on discrete probability distributions. Students should understand and use simple discrete distributions, with the binomial as a key example. In the US Common Core, standards S-MD.1, 3 and 4 ask students to define a random variable, graph its distribution, and find probabilities from theory or from data.","Key terms: sample space, outcome, discrete random variable, probability distribution (or probability function), P(X = x), cumulative probability P(X ≤ x) and relative frequency. A distribution is valid when no P(X = x) is below 0 and the probabilities sum to 1. A new variable from the same outcomes has a new distribution. For Y, the larger of two dice, P(Y = y) = (2y − 1)/36.","Common mistakes: assuming the values of X are equally likely, so P(X = 7) is taken as 1/11. Setting k = 1/n when P(X = x) = kx. Treating P(X < 4) as P(X ≤ 4). Accepting a negative probability because the list sums to 1. Calling a relative frequency the exact probability. Take care with notation as well: capital X is the variable and small x is one of its values.","In the lab the learner taps the 36 cells of a two-dice grid to build the distribution of the total. They slide k until kx sums to 1, and drag a cut to shade P(X ≤ 4), P(X < 4) and P(X ≥ 10). They roll 10, 100 and 1000 times and watch the relative frequencies settle near the model. Last, they build the distribution of the larger score from the same grid."]},"familyTip":"Roll two dice 50 times together and tally the totals. Before you start, each person bets on the most common total; then compare the tally with the 6/36 for seven.","keyIdea":"A random variable X gives a number to each outcome of an experiment, such as the total of two dice. Its probability distribution lists every value x with its probability P(X = x), in a table or a bar graph. Every probability is between 0 and 1, and together they sum to 1. You can build a distribution from theory, by counting equally likely outcomes, or estimate one from data using relative frequencies.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Could this list be a probability distribution? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✅ Valid distribution","❌ Not valid"],"items":[["0.2, 0.5, 0.3",0],["1/6 six times",0],["0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4",0],["1/36, 2/36, … 6/36, … 1/36 for 2 to 12",0],["0.4, 0.4, 0.4",1],["0.6, 0.5, −0.1",1],["0.25, 0.25, 0.25",1],["1.2, −0.2",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpdist","caption":"Flagship lab: build the two-dice total from 36 outcomes, slide k until kx sums to 1, shade ≤ and <, roll 1000 times, then build the larger score.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_cvIEWIClBr":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"In how many different orders can 5 different books be put on a shelf?","choices":["120","25","15"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply the choices for each position: 5 for the first, 4 for the second, and so on down to 1.","explain":"5! = 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 120 orders. 25 is 5 × 5, which would let one book be used twice."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A club has {n} members. In how many ways can it choose a president, a secretary and a treasurer, all different people?","answer":"n*(n-1)*(n-2)","hint":"The three jobs are different, so order matters. Multiply the choices for each job.","explain":"{n}P3 = {n} × {=n-1} × {=n-2} = {=n*(n-1)*(n-2)} ways.","params":{"n":[5,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"How many different groups of 3 can be chosen from {n} people?","answer":"n*(n-1)*(n-2)/6","hint":"Find {n}P3, then divide by 3! = 6, because each group can be ordered in 6 ways.","explain":"{n}C3 = {n} × {=n-1} × {=n-2} ÷ 6 = {=n*(n-1)*(n-2)/6} groups.","params":{"n":[4,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which of these is equal to 12C9?","choices":["12C3","12C4","12P9"],"answer":0,"hint":"Choosing r items to take is the same as choosing n − r items to leave.","explain":"nCr = nC(n − r). With n = 12 and r = 9, n − r = 3, so 12C9 = 12C3 = 220."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What is the value of 0!?","choices":["1","0","It is undefined"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use n! = n × (n − 1)! with n = 1.","explain":"1! = 1 × 0!, and 1! = 1, so 0! = 1. There is exactly one way to arrange nothing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"How many different arrangements are there of the letters of the word LEVEL?","choices":["30","60","120"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with 5!, then divide by the factorial of each repeated letter. Which letters repeat?","explain":"L appears twice and E appears twice, so 5! ÷ (2! × 2!) = 120 ÷ 4 = 30."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A lock uses 4 digits from 0 to 9, and digits may repeat. How many codes are possible?","choices":["10 000","5040","210"],"answer":0,"hint":"Order matters and repeats are allowed. How many choices does each digit have?","explain":"Each of the 4 places has 10 choices, so 10 × 10 × 10 × 10 = 10 000. 5040 = 10P4 forbids repeats and 210 = 10C4 ignores order."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why is nCr = nPr ÷ r!?","choices":["Each group of r appears r! times among the ordered lists","Combinations always have r! more options than permutations","Because you add r! extra orders to each group"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the 24 podiums and the 4 teams in the lab.","explain":"nPr counts ordered lists. Each unordered group can be put in r! orders, so it is counted r! times; dividing by r! counts each group once."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which situation is a combination, where order does not matter?","choices":["Choosing 3 people for a team","Giving out 1st, 2nd and 3rd prizes","Setting a 3-digit code"],"answer":0,"hint":"Swap two of the chosen items. Does it make a different result?","explain":"A team of A, B and C is the same in any order, so it is a combination. Prizes and codes change when you swap, so order matters there."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"In how many ways can a 1st and a 2nd prize be given to 2 different people from a group of {n}?","answer":"n*(n-1)","hint":"Count the choices for 1st prize, then the choices left for 2nd, and multiply.","explain":"{n} choices for 1st and {=n-1} for 2nd, so {n}P2 = {n} × {=n-1} = {=n*(n-1)} ways.","params":{"n":[5,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"How many ways are there to choose 2 people from {n}, when order does not matter?","answer":"n*(n-1)/2","hint":"Count ordered pairs first, then divide by 2!, the number of orders of each pair.","explain":"{n}P2 = {=n*(n-1)} ordered pairs, and each pair is counted 2! = 2 times, so {n}C2 = {=n*(n-1)/2}.","params":{"n":[4,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"How many different arrangements are there of the letters of the word EGG?","choices":["3","6","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with 3! for three letters, then allow for the two identical Gs.","explain":"3! = 6, but swapping the two Gs gives the same word, so divide by 2!: 6 ÷ 2 = 3 (EGG, GEG, GGE)."}],"explain":{"kid":["Four runners race for 1st, 2nd and 3rd. Any of the 4 can win. Then 3 are left for 2nd, and 2 for 3rd. So there are 4 × 3 × 2 = 24 podiums. When you make choices one after another, you multiply. This is the multiplication principle.","The number 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 is written 4! and said \"4 factorial\". It is the number of ways to put 4 different things in a row. So 3! = 6, 5! = 120 and 0! = 1. There is one way to arrange nothing.","An arrangement where order matters is a permutation. The 24 podiums are the permutations of 3 runners from 4, written 4P3. A team is different: a team of A, B and C is the same team in any order. Each team turns up 3! = 6 times among the 24 podiums. So there are 24 ÷ 6 = 4 teams. A choice where order does not matter is a combination, written 4C3.","Ask two questions every time. Does order matter? Can things repeat? A 3-digit code with repeats allowed has 10 × 10 × 10 = 1000 options. Identical letters cut the count. AAB has only 3 different orders, not 3! = 6, because swapping the two As changes nothing. Divide 3! by 2! to get 3."],"adult":["This topic sits in A level Mathematics, where students use n!, nCr and the notation (n r) in the binomial expansion and the binomial distribution. Some boards examine permutations and combinations directly in Further Mathematics or in statistics options. In the US Common Core, S-CP.9 (+) asks students to use permutations and combinations to work out probabilities of compound events.","Key terms: multiplication principle, factorial, permutation, combination, arrangement and selection. The formulas are nPr = n! ÷ (n − r)! and nCr = n! ÷ (r!(n − r)!). The symmetry nCr = nC(n − r) holds because choosing r to take is the same as choosing n − r to leave. Arrangements of n items with groups of p and q identical items number n! ÷ (p! q!).","Common mistakes: using nCr when order matters, such as for medals or codes. Using nPr for a committee, so each group is counted r! times. Using n to the power r when repeats are not allowed. Adding the stage counts instead of multiplying. Dividing by only one factorial when two letters repeat. Students should take care to treat 0! = 1 as a rule, since it makes nC0 = nCn = 1.","In the lab the learner fills a 1st, 2nd and 3rd podium from four runners and watches the choices fall from 4 to 3 to 2. They gather all 24 podiums into 4 teams to see nCr = nPr ÷ r!. They step n! from 0! to 8! on a shelf of books. They explore a row of nCr bars where r and n − r light up together. Last, they build every different order of AAB and AABB."]},"familyTip":"At dinner, count the ways your family could sit in a row of chairs, then the ways to pick two people to wash up. Which number is bigger, and why?","keyIdea":"To count choices made one after another, multiply the number of options at each stage. A permutation is an arrangement where order matters: nPr = n! ÷ (n − r)! ways to arrange r things from n. A combination is a selection where order does not matter: nCr = nPr ÷ r!, because each group of r can be ordered in r! ways. When some items are identical, divide by the factorial of each repeat.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does order matter? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🏅 Order matters: permutation","👥 Order does not matter: combination"],"items":[["Gold, silver and bronze among 8 runners",0],["A 4-digit PIN",0],["Seating 5 guests in a row",0],["Choosing a captain and a vice-captain",0],["A team of 5 from a class of 30",1],["A hand of 7 cards",1],["Picking 3 toppings for a pizza",1],["Choosing 6 lottery numbers",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagperm","caption":"Flagship lab: fill a podium from 4 runners, gather 24 podiums into teams, step n! down to 0!, explore nCr = nC(n − r), then arrange AAB and AABB.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_qKtu9QvDr9":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which of these can be modelled by a binomial distribution?","choices":["The number of heads in 15 flips of a fair coin","The number of flips until the first head","The score on one roll of a die"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check all four: fixed n, two outcomes, independent trials, constant p.","explain":"Fifteen flips gives a fixed n, heads or tails, independent flips and p = 0.5. The second has no fixed n. The third has six outcomes."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A multiple-choice test has {n} questions, each with 4 options. A student guesses every answer. X is the number correct, X ~ B({n}, p). What is p?","answer":"0.25","hint":"On each question, how many of the options are right?","explain":"Each guess is correct with probability 1/4 = 0.25, whatever n is. So X ~ B({n}, 0.25).","params":{"n":[10,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A school has 1200 students. 20 are picked at random without replacement. X is the number who walk to school. Is B(20, p) reasonable?","choices":["Yes: the sample is small next to the population, so p barely changes","No: without replacement can never be binomial","No: there are more than two outcomes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the sample size with the population size.","explain":"20 is under 2% of 1200, so removing each student hardly changes p. The trials are close to independent, and B(20, p) is a good approximation."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"X is the number of days it rains in the next 7 days. Which assumption is most doubtful?","choices":["Independent trials","A fixed number of trials","Two outcomes per trial"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does rain today make rain tomorrow more likely?","explain":"Weather runs in spells, so one rainy day makes the next one more likely. That breaks independence, and p may also change across the week."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A population of {=10*n} items is sampled without replacement. Using the 10% condition, what is the largest sample size for which a binomial model is reasonable?","answer":"n","hint":"The sample should be at most 10% of the population.","explain":"10% of {=10*n} is {n}. A sample of {n} or fewer keeps p close to constant.","params":{"n":[20,90]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"X counts the faulty phones in a sample of 25, and 4% are faulty. Which statement defines the model correctly?","choices":["X ~ B(25, 0.04)","X ~ B(0.04, 25)","X ~ B(25, 0.96)"],"answer":0,"hint":"In B(n, p), n is the number of trials and p is the chance of a success. Here a success is a faulty phone.","explain":"n = 25 phones and p = 0.04, because X counts faulty ones. B(25, 0.96) would count phones that work."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A spinner has red, green and blue sections. X is the number of reds in 10 spins. Can X be binomial?","choices":["Yes, if success is red and failure is not red","No, because there are three colours","No, because spins are not independent"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you group the outcomes into exactly two?","explain":"Each spin is red or not red, which is two outcomes. With 10 independent spins and the same P(red), X ~ B(10, P(red))."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A bag has 5 red and 5 blue counters. Two are drawn with replacement. X is the number of reds. What is p for each draw?","answer":0.5,"hint":"With replacement, the bag is the same for every draw.","explain":"Each draw has 5 reds out of 10, so p = 1/2 every time. The draws are independent and X ~ B(2, 0.5)."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A fair die is rolled until a six appears. X is the number of rolls. Why is X not binomial?","choices":["The number of trials is not fixed","There are more than two outcomes","The probability of a six changes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask how many trials there will be before you start. Do you know?","explain":"You keep rolling until a six, so n is not fixed in advance. Each roll still has two outcomes (six or not six) with p = 1/6."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"In a large batch, {k}% of bulbs are faulty. X is the number of bulbs that work in a sample of 20. X ~ B(20, p). What is p as a decimal?","answer":"(100-k)/100","hint":"X counts bulbs that work, not faulty ones. Success is a working bulb.","explain":"A bulb works with probability 1 − {k}/100 = {=(100-k)/100}. So X ~ B(20, {=(100-k)/100}). Using {=k/100} would count faulty bulbs instead.","params":{"k":[2,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A bag holds 3 red and 7 blue counters. You take 4 out, one at a time, keeping each one out. Which condition fails?","choices":["Independence and a constant p","A fixed number of trials","Two outcomes per trial"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out P(red) on the second draw after a red came out first.","explain":"After a red is removed, P(red) falls from 3/10 to 2/9. The draws affect each other, so they are not independent and p is not constant."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A bag holds {r} red and {=11-r} blue counters. You draw one, keep it out, and it is red. What is P(red) on the next draw, as a decimal?","answer":"(r-1)/10","hint":"One red has gone. Count the reds left and the counters left.","explain":"There are {=r-1} reds left out of 10 counters, so P(red) = {=(r-1)/10}. It was {r}/11 at the start, so p has changed.","params":{"r":[2,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A binomial model counts successes. You do a trial again and again. Each time, it is a success or a failure. X is the number of successes. We write X ~ B(n, p). Here n is the number of trials and p is the chance of a success on each one.","The model only fits when four things are true. First, n is fixed before you start. Second, each trial has just two outcomes. Third, the trials are independent, so one result does not change the next. Fourth, p stays the same on every trial. Check all four. If one fails, B(n, p) is the wrong model.","Watch out for \"until\". Rolling a die until you get a six has no fixed n, so it is not binomial. A score from 1 to 6 has six outcomes, not two. But \"is it a six?\" has two, so it can be binomial. Picking from a bag and keeping the counter out changes p each time. Then the trials are not independent.","Big groups help. Say a factory makes 10 000 bulbs and 10% are faulty. Take out 20 bulbs to test. Each one you remove barely moves the 10%. So B(20, 0.1) is a very good fit, even without replacement. With only 100 bulbs, p moves a lot, and the model is weaker."],"adult":["This topic sits in the A level Mathematics content on the binomial distribution. Students should understand and use the binomial distribution as a model, and say when it is a suitable one. In the US, AP Statistics teaches the same checks as BINS (binary, independent, number fixed, same probability). It also adds the 10% condition: sampling without replacement is treated as independent when the sample is at most 10% of the population.","Key terms: trial, Bernoulli trial, success, failure, independent, random variable, X ~ B(n, p), with replacement, without replacement, population, sample, assumption and approximation. Students should state the model in context. For example: \"X is the number of faulty bulbs in a sample of 20, X ~ B(20, 0.1).\" They should also say which assumptions it rests on.","Common mistakes: using B(n, p) when n is not fixed, as in \"roll until a six\". Using it for counts with more than two outcomes, such as the score on a die. Taking p as the chance of failure when X counts successes. Ignoring sampling without replacement from a small group. Assuming independence for linked events, such as days of rain in a row or one family's children.","In the lab the learner lights the four condition lamps for three contexts. They draw counters from a bag with and without replacement and watch P(red) move. They slide the population size until 20 draws without replacement keep p within 0.09 to 0.11. Then they find the one broken condition in four contexts, and define X ~ B(n, p) by dragging n and picking p."]},"familyTip":"Take a pack of cards and draw five, keeping each card out. Say the chance the next card is a heart before each draw. Then do it again, putting each card back and shuffling. Talk about which version is binomial and why.","keyIdea":"A count X follows a binomial distribution, X ~ B(n, p), only when four conditions hold: a fixed number of trials n, two outcomes per trial (success or failure), independent trials, and the same probability of success p on every trial. Before you use B(n, p), check each condition in the context. Sampling without replacement breaks independence, but when the population is large compared with the sample, B(n, p) is a good approximation.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is B(n, p) a good model for X? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✅ Binomial fits","❌ Not binomial"],"items":[["X = heads in 12 fair coin flips",0],["X = sixes in 30 rolls of a die",0],["X = faulty items in 20 picked from 50 000",0],["X = correct guesses on 10 four-choice questions",0],["X = rolls until the first six",1],["X = the score on one die",1],["X = red cards in 5 drawn from 8, kept out",1],["X = rainy days in a row next week",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbfit","caption":"Flagship lab: light the four conditions, draw with and without replacement, slide the population size, find the broken condition, then define X ~ B(n, p).","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Shb7qv8HhX":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"You guess all 10 questions on a test. Each has 4 options. What is P(exactly 5 right), to 3 significant figures?","choices":["0.0584","0.000977","0.5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use 10C5 × 0.25⁵ × 0.75⁵.","explain":"10C5 × 0.25⁵ × 0.75⁵ = 252 × 0.000977 × 0.2373 ≈ 0.0584. Guessing half right is rare."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why do in, in, miss, miss and in, miss, in, miss have the same probability?","choices":["Each has the same number of p and 1 − p factors","The first is more likely, as hits come together","Their chances add to 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write each as a product of four numbers.","explain":"Both are p × p × (1 − p) × (1 − p) in some order. Multiplying in a new order gives the same product, so every order has p² (1 − p)²."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A coin is flipped {n} times. How many different head-tail orders are there in total?","answer":"2**n","hint":"Each flip has 2 outcomes. Multiply for each flip.","explain":"Each flip doubles the count, so there are 2^{n} = {=2**n} orders. They split into nC0 + nC1 + … + nCn, one group for each number of heads.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"X ~ B(8, 0.3). What is P(X ≥ 1)?","choices":["1 − 0.7⁸","1 − 0.3⁸","8 × 0.3"],"answer":0,"hint":"The only value left out is X = 0. What is P(X = 0)?","explain":"P(X = 0) means all 8 fail, so it is 0.7⁸ ≈ 0.0576. Then P(X ≥ 1) = 1 − 0.7⁸ ≈ 0.942."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You want P(X ≥ {k}) as 1 − P(X ≤ c). What is c?","answer":"k-1","hint":"'At least {k}' keeps {k}. The other side stops just below it.","explain":"The values left out of X ≥ {k} are 0 up to {=k-1}. So P(X ≥ {k}) = 1 − P(X ≤ {=k-1}).","params":{"k":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"X ~ B(5, 0.6). Which is P(X = 2)?","choices":["5C2 × 0.6² × 0.4³","5C2 × 0.6³ × 0.4²","5 × 0.6 × 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"The power on p is the number of successes.","explain":"2 successes give 0.6², and 3 failures give 0.4³. With 5C2 = 10 orders, P(X = 2) = 10 × 0.36 × 0.064 = 0.2304."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"X ~ B(10, 0.25). Which value of X is most likely?","choices":["2","5","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"The mean is np = 2.5. The tallest bar is near it.","explain":"P(X = 2) ≈ 0.2816 is the tallest bar, just ahead of P(X = 3) ≈ 0.2503. Getting 5 right is only about 0.0584."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"X ~ B(20, 0.1). Which is P(X > 4)?","choices":["1 − P(X ≤ 4)","1 − P(X ≤ 3)","P(X ≤ 4)"],"answer":0,"hint":"'More than 4' does not include 4.","explain":"X > 4 means 5 or more. The values left out are 0 to 4, so P(X > 4) = 1 − P(X ≤ 4)."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Binomial probabilities: X ~ B(4, 0.7). Which is P(X = 2)?","choices":["4C2 × 0.7² × 0.3²","0.7² × 0.3²","4P2 × 0.7² × 0.3²"],"answer":0,"hint":"One order has chance 0.7² × 0.3². How many orders are there?","explain":"Each order of 2 successes and 2 failures has chance 0.7² × 0.3² = 0.0441. There are 4C2 = 6 orders, so P(X = 2) = 0.2646."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"X ~ B({n}, p). How many different orders give exactly 2 successes? (This is nC2.)","answer":"n*(n-1)/2","hint":"Choose which 2 of the n trials are the successes: nC2 = n × (n − 1) ÷ 2.","explain":"{n}C2 = {n} × {=n-1} ÷ 2 = {=n*(n-1)/2} orders, and each has the same chance p² (1 − p)^{=n-2}.","params":{"n":[4,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"X ~ B(10, 0.25). Which is P(X ≥ 3)?","choices":["1 − P(X ≤ 2)","1 − P(X ≤ 3)","P(X ≤ 3)"],"answer":0,"hint":"'At least 3' keeps 3. Which values are left over?","explain":"The values not in X ≥ 3 are 0, 1 and 2. So P(X ≥ 3) = 1 − P(X ≤ 2) ≈ 1 − 0.5256 = 0.4744."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"X ~ B({n}, 0.3). In P(X = {r}) = {n}C{r} × 0.3^{r} × 0.7^k, what is the power k?","answer":"n-r","hint":"The power on 1 − p is the number of failures.","explain":"There are {r} successes out of {n} trials, so {n} − {r} = {=n-r} failures. The power on 0.7 is {=n-r}.","params":{"n":[8,15],"r":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Say a player scores 70% of her shots, and she takes 4. What is the chance that exactly 2 go in? Start with one order: in, in, miss, miss. The shots are independent, so we multiply. That gives 0.7 × 0.7 × 0.3 × 0.3 = 0.0441.","But there are other orders, like in, miss, in, miss. Each one has two 0.7s and two 0.3s. So each one has the same chance, 0.0441. We just need to count the orders. There are 4C2 = 6 of them, so the answer is 6 × 0.0441 = 0.2646.","That is the whole rule. P(X = r) = nCr × p to the power r × (1 − p) to the power n − r. The r successes give p to the power r. The n − r failures give 1 − p to the power n − r. Then nCr counts the orders.","For 'at least' or 'at most', add up bars. Often the other side is shorter. P(X ≥ 3) is 1 − P(X ≤ 2). Note the 2: 'at least 3' keeps 3 itself, so you take away 0, 1 and 2."],"adult":["This topic matches the A level Mathematics statistics content: calculate probabilities using the binomial distribution, including cumulative probabilities. In the US, AP Statistics and the Common Core standard S-MD.3 ask students to build and use the distribution of a count of successes. Students should find P(X = r), P(X ≤ r) and P(X ≥ r) by hand and with a calculator, and state each answer in context.","Key terms: binomial distribution, X ~ B(n, p), trial, success, probability of success p, P(X = r), probability distribution, binomial coefficient nCr, cumulative probability P(X ≤ r), complement, and mean np. Checking that the model fits is a separate lesson. Here we take B(n, p) as given and work out probabilities.","Common mistakes: leaving out nCr and finding the chance of one order only. Using nPr, which counts each set of successes many times. Writing p × r for p to the power r. Swapping the powers, so p gets n − r. Most often, slips at the edge: P(X ≥ 4) is 1 − P(X ≤ 3), not 1 − P(X ≤ 4). Be careful too with 'more than' and 'fewer than', which leave out the end value.","In the lab the learner finds all six orders of two hits in four shots and sees each has the same chance. They build the formula from tiles with traps such as nPr. Then they read the whole B(10, 0.25) chart and shade bars for 'at least', 'at most' and 'more than' events."]},"familyTip":"Guess the answers to a 10-question quiz with 4 options each. The chance of exactly 5 right is 10C5 × 0.25^5 × 0.75^5, about 0.058, so about 6 in 100. Ask your learner why the most likely score is 2, not 5.","keyIdea":"If X ~ B(n, p), the chance of exactly r successes is P(X = r) = nCr × p^r × (1 − p)^(n − r). The power p^r covers the r successes, (1 − p)^(n − r) covers the n − r failures, and nCr counts the orders they can come in, since every order has the same chance. For ranges, add bars or use the complement: P(X ≥ k) = 1 − P(X ≤ k − 1).","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"X ~ B(10, 0.25). Which cumulative form gives each event? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["P(X ≤ k)","1 − P(X ≤ k − 1)"],"items":[["At most 2 right",0],["No more than 4 right",0],["Fewer than 3 right",0],["At least 3 right",1],["More than 4 right",1],["5 or more right",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbinp","caption":"Flagship lab: count orders of 2 hits in 4 shots, see each has equal chance, build nCr p^r (1 − p)^(n − r) from tiles, then read and shade B(10, 0.25).","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_QtfZ4htHBD":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"X ~ B(20, 0.85). Which describes the shape?","choices":["Negative skew, tail to the left","Positive skew, tail to the right","Symmetric about 10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Large p means mostly successes. Which side has the thin tail?","explain":"With p = 0.85 the bars pile up near np = 17 and the tail runs left, so it has negative skew."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"X ~ B(4, 0.1). What is the mode?","choices":["0","0.4","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"The mode is a whole number at or next to np.","explain":"np = 0.4, and the mode is ⌊(n + 1)p⌋ = ⌊0.5⌋ = 0. The mode must be a value X can take, so it is 0, not 0.4."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which has the larger standard deviation?","choices":["B(40, 0.5)","B(40, 0.1)","They are equal, as n is the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use σ = √(np(1 − p)) for each one and compare the two numbers.","explain":"B(40, 0.5) has σ = √10 ≈ 3.16, and B(40, 0.1) has σ = √3.6 ≈ 1.90. The spread is largest when p = 0.5."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"p = 0.1 is fixed and n goes from 10 to 100. What happens to the skew?","choices":["It gets weaker","It gets stronger","It flips to negative"],"answer":0,"hint":"The skew measure is (1 − 2p)/√(np(1 − p)). What happens as n grows?","explain":"It gets weaker. The top of (1 − 2p)/√(np(1 − p)) stays the same, but the bottom grows with n, so the chart looks more like a bell."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A chart has 11 bars, a peak at 8 and a long tail to the left. Which model fits?","choices":["B(10, 0.8)","B(10, 0.2)","B(11, 0.5)"],"answer":0,"hint":"11 bars means n = 10. Where is np?","explain":"B(10, 0.8): 11 bars means n = 10, the peak at 8 fits np = 8, and a left tail means p > 0.5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"X ~ B({=4*n}, 0.25). What is the mean?","answer":"n","hint":"The mean of a binomial is np: multiply the trials by p.","explain":"np = {=4*n} × 0.25 = {n}. The mode is at or next to {n}.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"X ~ B({n}, 0.5). What is the variance np(1 − p), times 4?","answer":"n","hint":"np(1 − p) = n × 0.5 × 0.5 = n ÷ 4. Multiply that by 4.","explain":"The variance is {n} × 0.5 × 0.5 = {n} ÷ 4, and 4 times that is {n}.","params":{"n":[4,16]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Is it true that the peak of B(n, p) is always at n/2?","choices":["No, it is at or next to np","Yes, the middle is always tallest","Only when n is even"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where does the mean np sit?","explain":"No. The peak is at or next to np, which is n/2 only when p = 0.5."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Two charts have mean 4: B(40, 0.1) and B(8, 0.5). How do they differ?","choices":["B(40, 0.1) is skewed and wider","They are identical","B(8, 0.5) is skewed to the right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same mean; compare σ and the value of p.","explain":"B(40, 0.1) has σ = √3.6 ≈ 1.90 and positive skew. B(8, 0.5) has σ = √2 ≈ 1.41 and is symmetric."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"X ~ B(15, 0.2). Which describes the shape?","choices":["Positive skew, tail to the right","Negative skew, tail to the left","Symmetric about 7.5"],"answer":0,"hint":"With p small, most trials fail. Where do most of the bars pile up?","explain":"With p = 0.2 the bars pile up near np = 3 and the tail runs right, so it has positive skew. Skew is named after the tail."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"X ~ B({n}, 0.5). About which value is the distribution symmetric?","answer":"n/2","hint":"With p = 0.5 the chart is symmetric about its mean np.","explain":"The mean is np = {n} × 0.5 = {=n/2}, and with p = 0.5 the chart is symmetric about {=n/2}.","params":{"n":[4,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How are B(10, 0.3) and B(10, 0.7) related?","choices":["Mirror images: P(X = x) for one equals P(X = 10 − x) for the other","They are the same distribution","B(10, 0.7) is the same shape moved right by 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count failures instead of successes.","explain":"They are mirror images about 5. Counting failures swaps p and 1 − p, so P(X = x) becomes P(X = 10 − x)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"X ~ B({=10*n}, 0.3). What is the mean np?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Multiply the number of trials by p.","explain":"np = {=10*n} × 0.3 = {=3*n}. The peak of the chart is at or next to {=3*n}.","params":{"n":[1,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Say you flip a fair coin 10 times and count heads. That count is X ~ B(10, 0.5). The bar chart has 11 bars, for 0 up to 10. It is tallest at 5 and falls away evenly on both sides, so it is symmetric.","Now say only 1 shot in 5 goes in, and you take 10 shots: X ~ B(10, 0.2). The mean is np = 10 × 0.2 = 2. Most of the bars near 0, 1 and 2 are tall. Then a thin tail runs off to the right. We call that positive skew. If p were 0.8, the chart would flip, with the tail on the left. That is negative skew.","The peak is the mode, the most likely value. It is always a whole number at or next to np. For B(10, 0.3), np = 3 and the mode is 3. For B(4, 0.1), np = 0.4, so the mode is 0.","The spread is the standard deviation, σ = √(np(1 − p)). It is largest when p = 0.5. If you keep p fixed and make n bigger, the mean moves right, the chart gets wider, and the skew gets weaker. The chart starts to look like a bell."],"adult":["This topic matches the A level Mathematics statistics content on the binomial distribution, where students should be able to describe and sketch its shape and use it to model counts. AP Statistics asks students to describe the shape, centre and spread of a binomial distribution, with mean np and standard deviation √(np(1 − p)). The International Baccalaureate Mathematics courses use the same results. A useful exact fact is that the mode is ⌊(n + 1)p⌋, or both (n + 1)p and (n + 1)p − 1 when (n + 1)p is a whole number.","The most common mistake is to get the skew direction backwards. Students see the bars piled up on the left and call it negative or left skew. Skew is named after the tail, not the pile, so small p gives positive (right) skew. A second mistake is to think the peak sits in the middle of 0 to n. It sits near np, and only at n/2 when p = 0.5.","Students also often miss that B(n, p) and B(n, 1 − p) are reflections of each other. Counting failures instead of successes swaps p and 1 − p, so P(X = x) = P(Y = n − x). The skew measure (1 − 2p)/√(np(1 − p)) shows why the skew fades as n grows with p fixed, which links to the normal approximation later in the course.","In the lab your learner taps where they think each chart peaks before the bars appear. They drag p and see positive skew, symmetry and negative skew in turn. They drag p to mirror an outline of B(10, p₀), then grow n and hit mean targets while watching σ and skew. Last, they name unlabelled charts, including two with the same mean but different spread."]},"familyTip":"Ask your learner to sketch B(10, 0.2) and B(10, 0.8) side by side without a calculator, then explain which one has positive skew and why. Check with a calculator's binomial table: the two lists of probabilities should be the same, but in reverse order.","keyIdea":"The shape of X ~ B(n, p) is set by n and p. The peak (mode) sits at or next to the mean np. When p < 0.5 the chart has positive skew, with a long tail to the right; p = 0.5 gives a symmetric chart; p > 0.5 gives negative skew. B(n, 1 − p) is the mirror image of B(n, p). As n grows, the spread √(np(1 − p)) grows and the skew fades.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which shape does each binomial have? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Positive skew","Symmetric","Negative skew"],"items":[["B(12, 0.1)",0],["B(30, 0.25)",0],["B(8, 0.5)",1],["B(15, 0.5)",1],["B(10, 0.9)",2],["B(20, 0.65)",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbshape","caption":"Flagship lab: guess each peak before the bars appear, slide p through the three skews, mirror B(n, p), grow n and name unlabelled charts.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_iFFKZd-Vgv":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Where does 'certain' go on the likelihood scale?","choices":["at 1","at 0","at ½"],"answer":0,"hint":"The scale runs from impossible to certain.","explain":"Certain is at 1, the end of the scale. Impossible is at 0 and even chance at ½."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A fair coin is tossed. How likely is tails?","choices":["even chance","likely","certain"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many sides does a coin have, and are they equally likely?","explain":"Heads and tails are equally likely, so tails is 1 out of 2: an even chance."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Bag A has 2 red out of 4 counters. Bag B has 2 red out of 10. Which gives the better chance of red?","choices":["bag A","bag B","the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the red with the total in each bag, not just the red.","explain":"2 out of 4 is half, an even chance. 2 out of 10 is unlikely. Bag A is better."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A bag has {a} red counters and no others. How many counters must you add to make red an even chance?","answer":"a","hint":"Even chance means as many other counters as red ones.","explain":"Add {a} blue counters: {a} red out of {=a*2} is half, an even chance.","params":{"a":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A spinner is likely to land on red. Which of these could happen in 5 spins?","choices":["red 4 times, blue once","red every time is the only possible result","blue every time is impossible to see"],"answer":0,"hint":"Likely means probably, not always.","explain":"Red should come up most often, but blue can still appear, so 4 red and 1 blue is quite possible."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which event is unlikely but possible?","choices":["rolling a 6 on a normal dice","rolling a 9 on a normal dice","rolling a number from 1 to 6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the event that could happen but probably will not.","explain":"A 6 is 1 out of 6, so unlikely but possible. A 9 is impossible and 1 to 6 is certain."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A bag has {=a+b} counters: {a} green and {b} pink. How many more green counters than pink are there?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the pink away from the green.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} more green. With more green than pink, picking green is likely.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A coin lands on tails five times in a row. How likely is heads on the next toss?","choices":["even chance","certain","impossible"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the coin remember what happened before?","explain":"Each toss is fresh, so heads is still 1 out of 2: an even chance. A run of tails does not make heads due."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A normal dice is rolled. How likely is it to show a 0?","choices":["impossible","unlikely","even chance"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which numbers are on a normal dice?","explain":"A dice shows 1 to 6 only, so 0 can never come up. That is impossible, at 0 on the scale."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A bag has {=a*2} counters. How many of them must be red for an even chance of picking red?","answer":"a","hint":"Even chance means half of the counters.","explain":"Half of {=a*2} is {a}, so {a} red counters give an even chance.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A spinner has {=a+b} equal parts. {a} are red and the rest are blue. How many parts are blue?","answer":"b","hint":"Take the red parts away from all the parts.","explain":"{=a+b} − {a} = {b} blue parts. Compare red with all {=a+b} parts to judge the chance.","params":{"a":[5,9],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A spinner has 7 red parts and 1 blue part, all the same size. Landing on red is…","choices":["likely","certain","unlikely"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is red more than half? Could it ever land on blue?","explain":"7 out of 8 is more than half, so red is likely. Blue can still come up, so red is not certain."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some things will happen for sure. Some things can never happen. Most things are in between. We use chance words to say how likely they are.","Impossible means it can never happen, like a dice showing 7. Certain means it will happen for sure. Even chance is half and half, like heads on a coin.","Unlikely means it could happen, but probably not. Likely means it will probably happen. Be careful: likely is not always. A likely spinner can still land on blue.","We can put the words on a line. Impossible is at 0. Even chance is in the middle, at one half. Certain is at 1. Unlikely and likely go in between."],"adult":["This topic builds the language of chance before probability is written as a number. Pupils order events on a likelihood scale using impossible, unlikely, even chance, likely and certain. At Key Stage 3 the same line becomes the probability scale from 0 to 1. Pupils then record outcomes and use fractions for the chance of each one.","The key move is to compare the outcomes you want with all the possible outcomes. A bag with 4 red counters out of 8 gives an even chance of red. A bag with 4 red out of 20 makes red unlikely, even though the number of red counters is the same. This only works when each outcome is equally likely, such as equal parts of a spinner or counters of the same size.","Common mistakes: many children count only the red counters, so they think 2 red out of 8 is the same chance as 2 red out of 4. Others treat likely as certain. They are then surprised when a mostly red spinner lands on blue. Some call any rare event impossible. Some think a result is due after a run of misses, such as heads after five tails.","In the lab your child places 'impossible' at 0 for a bag with no red. They paint counters red until red is an even chance, then until it is unlikely but still possible. They make red likely on a spinner and spin it 10 times. Last, they place two bags with the same number of red on the scale."]},"familyTip":"Put some coloured socks or bricks in a bag. Before each pick, agree on a chance word for 'we get a blue one' and point to it on a line drawn from impossible to certain. Pick, put it back, and repeat ten times. Then change what is in the bag and see if the word changes.","keyIdea":"Chance words describe how likely an event is: impossible, unlikely, even chance, likely and certain. They sit in order on a likelihood scale from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain), with even chance at ½ in the middle. To judge the chance, compare the outcomes you want with all the equally likely outcomes, not just count the ones you want. Likely means it will probably happen, not that it always will, and unlikely means it could still happen.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"How likely is it? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Impossible","Unlikely","Likely","Certain"],"items":[["Rolling a 7 on a normal dice",0],["A cat growing wings and flying",0],["Rolling a 6 on a normal dice",1],["Picking the one pink sweet from a bag of 10",1],["Rolling a number bigger than 1 on a dice",2],["Picking green from 9 green and 1 pink",2],["The day after Monday being Tuesday",3],["Rolling a number from 1 to 6 on a dice",3]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaglikely","caption":"Flagship lab: drag a pointer along the 0-to-1 likelihood scale, paint counters and spinner parts red, spin 10 times and compare two bags.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_J4j7d3iAfg":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A fair dice is rolled {=6*a} times. About how many even numbers do you expect?","answer":"3*a","hint":"Three of the six faces are even (2, 4 and 6), so the chance is 3 in 6, which is one half.","explain":"Expected evens = {=6*a} × 3/6 = {=3*a}.","params":{"a":[2,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which experiment gives a share of heads you can trust most?","choices":["10 flips","50 flips","1000 flips"],"answer":2,"hint":"More trials make the share steadier.","explain":"With 1000 flips the share of heads settles close to one half. Small samples wobble a lot."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A spinner has 3 equal parts: red, blue and green. In 30 spins, about how many reds do you expect?","choices":["10","15","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Red is 1 of 3 equal parts. Multiply the spins by one third.","explain":"30 × 1/3 = 10. Choosing 15 treats it as 'red or not red, so 50-50', which uses outcomes, not chances."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A dice is rolled 60 times and shows a 6 twenty times. What is the best conclusion?","choices":["It is probably unfair: about 10 sixes were expected","It is surely fair: anything can happen","Sixes are always more common"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out the expected number of sixes in 60 rolls first.","explain":"A fair dice gives about 60 ÷ 6 = 10 sixes. Twenty is double, which is too far off to be luck in 60 rolls. More rolls would make the case stronger."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A fair coin is flipped 50 times and lands heads {a} times. How many more heads is that than the expected number?","answer":"a-25","hint":"First find the expected heads: 50 × ½ = 25. Then subtract 25 from {a}.","explain":"Expected heads = 25, so {a} is {=a-25} more. A gap like this is normal in 50 flips; as a share it shrinks when there are more trials.","params":{"a":[26,34]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You pull from a bag 40 times, putting each counter back. 30 are red. The bag has 4 counters. How many are probably red?","choices":["3","2","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Red came up 30 out of 40 times. Which fraction of 4 is the same?","explain":"30 out of 40 is 3 in 4, so about 3 of the 4 counters are red. All 4 would make every pull red."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why do we put the counter back after each pull?","choices":["So every pull has the same chances","So the bag gets heavier","So red comes up more"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what happens to the chances if counters are taken out and kept.","explain":"Putting it back keeps the bag the same for every trial, so each pull has the same chance of red."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"After six tails in a row, Sam says, 'The next one has to be heads.' What is the slip?","choices":["The coin has no memory; it is still 1 in 2","Sam is right: heads is due","Tails will always win now"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do past flips change the coin?","explain":"Each flip is a fresh trial with the same 1 in 2 chance. Believing heads is due is the gambler's fallacy."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A fair coin is flipped {=2*a} times. About how many heads do you expect?","answer":"a","hint":"Heads is 1 of 2 equally likely outcomes. Multiply the number of flips by one half.","explain":"Expected heads = {=2*a} × ½ = {a}. The real count will usually be close to {a}, not always exactly {a}.","params":{"a":[5,40]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A fair dice is rolled {=6*a} times. About how many times do you expect a 4?","answer":"a","hint":"A dice has 6 equally likely faces, so a 4 comes up 1 time in 6. Divide the rolls by 6.","explain":"Expected 4s = {=6*a} × 1/6 = {a}. Each of the six faces has the same expected count.","params":{"a":[2,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A coin lands on heads four times in a row. What is true about the next flip?","choices":["Tails is now more likely","Heads and tails are still equally likely","Heads is now more likely"],"answer":1,"hint":"Ask yourself: can the coin remember what it did before?","explain":"Each flip is separate. The coin has no memory, so the chance of heads is still 1 in 2. Thinking a tail is 'due' is called the gambler's fallacy."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Mia flips a coin 10 times and gets 7 heads. What is the best conclusion?","choices":["The coin must be unfair","The coin could still be fair: 10 flips is a small sample","Heads will keep coming up more often"],"answer":1,"hint":"How far can 10 flips wobble from 5 heads just by luck?","explain":"Small samples often land away from the expected count. 7 heads in 10 is quite common for a fair coin. Many more flips are needed to judge fairness."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A bag has {a} red and {b} blue counters. You pull one, note it, and put it back, {=10*(a+b)} times. About how many reds do you expect?","answer":"10*a","hint":"The chance of red is red counters ÷ all counters. Multiply that by the number of pulls.","explain":"Chance of red = {a}/{=a+b}. Expected reds = {=10*(a+b)} × {a}/{=a+b} = {=10*a}.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[1,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["An experiment is a test you repeat. Each go is called a trial, and what happens is the outcome. Flip a coin: the outcome is heads or tails. Keep a tally so you do not lose count.","To find the expected number, multiply the trials by the chance. A coin gives heads 1 time in 2, so 20 flips should give about 10 heads. A dice shows each number 1 time in 6, so 30 rolls should give about 5 sixes.","Real results wobble. You might get 8 heads or 12 heads, and that is normal. After four heads in a row, a tail is not due. The coin does not remember. The next flip is still 1 in 2.","More trials make the result steadier. Ten flips can give 7 heads. A thousand flips give close to half heads. So if a dice shows 20 sixes in 60 rolls, when about 10 were expected, it is probably not fair."],"adult":["Ages 9 to 10. This is the hands-on side of chance. It fits beside Year 5 and 6 statistics in England and leads into KS3 work on comparing results from trials with what theory predicts. It builds on Likelihood Language and on tally charts.","Key words: trial, outcome, tally, expected frequency and relative frequency. Expected frequency is the number of trials times the chance, so 60 rolls give about 10 sixes. Relative frequency is the count divided by the trials. It is a fair estimate of the chance, and it gets better as the trials grow. Even so, a small sample can land far from what you expect.","Common mistakes: expecting exactly 10 heads in 20 flips; thinking a tail is due after a run of heads (the gambler's fallacy); thinking a streak will go on (the hot hand); saying two colours must mean 50-50; and judging a dice from a few rolls. Be careful to say about 10, not 10.","In the lab your child flips 20 times and compares with 10. They test 100 flips after four heads in a row, and roll 30 times against 5 per face. They watch the share of heads settle over 1000 flips, guess a hidden bag from 40 pulls, and judge a loaded dice from 60 rolls."]},"familyTip":"Flip a coin 20 times together and keep a tally. Before you start, each guess how many heads. Then pool everyone's flips: is the bigger total closer to half?","keyIdea":"A chance experiment repeats a trial, like a coin flip or a dice roll, and records each outcome in a tally. The expected number is trials × chance: 20 flips × ½ = 10 heads. Real results wobble around it, the coin has no memory of a streak, and more trials give a share closer to the expected one.","visual":{"type":"problab","caption":"Flip a coin again and again. Watch the share of heads wobble, then settle near one half.","opts":{"kind":"coin"}},"lab":{"type":"flagchance","caption":"Flagship lab: flip, roll and pull. Compare your results with what you expected, test a streak, and judge a suspicious dice.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
